WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.210 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by Daniel  Abraham who is half of the writing team  James sa Corey on the expanse novels as  well as co-executive producer on the  television program based on those novels  Daniel it's so great to talk to you I'm  a fan of the program and I've since  gotten into to the novels on which the  program is based you know we recently  spoke to Ben Mezrich known for of course  the book bringing down the house  ultimately turned into the movie 21 the  book on which the film the social  network was based and we talked a little  bit about just sort of the process of  what it's like to have something that he  wrote turned into in his case a movie in  your case a television program talk to  us a little bit generally about how that  works in other words you've developed  this universe this this environment and  characters in which these stories are  told and then the process begins for  turning it turning it into a TV show  what's the sort of process by which that  happens well the first thing of course  happens is that we find somebody who's  willing to actually do the work and  produce the money which is the  equivalent of winning several lotteries  in a row so that that's getting blind  lucky is the first part and then you  find out kind of who you're working with  we were very lucky because we wound up  paired with Mark Ferguson Hawk Ostby the  guys who wrote children of men and Iron  Man and Alcon entertainment which is the  the company that bought the rights and  brought us all together and Mark and  Hawk were very kind in that they  insisted on bringing us into the writing  room with them and with the team so at  that point we were all of us in there  together  mark showrunner Narayan Shankar was kind  of presiding over us as we rethought the  whole story that we'd written in a way  that would use the toolset for visual  media and when we actually kind of built  it to not be film about hmm and are  there situations where there there were  just aspects of the the books  that just kind of couldn't be replicated  visually and the story had to be  modified as a result of that absolutely  especially in the first book Leviathan  wakes we have a character who is very  introspective and very much lives in his  own head Miller if you're really just  filmed what Miller did in the first book  you'd have a guy sitting in a room  drinking whiskey for a long time and it  would not be compelling television what  are the issues with co-authoring I mean  I can't even imagine like when you when  you guys sit down to write or I guess do  you sit down together to write our  chapters divvied up are certain like the  somebody do dialog and somebody else  does the the non dialogue parts or how  do you even do that it varies a lot  depending on a quick kind which  individual collaboration you're working  with this is one collaboration that I've  done but I've done I've done others with  other people and they all have different  shapes the way that ty and I work  together ty Frank's the other half of  James sa Corey we outline the whole  project together the whole book and then  outline each individual chapter together  so each of us knows what's going to be  in a given chapter and we also have this  rule about how long a chapter is which  makes it really kind of as a gameplay  thing much easier to do then I'll write  one chapter  Tyler write another chapter we'll swap  each of us will edit what the other guy  did we'll put her in the back of a  master document then when the master  document is done one of us will go all  the way through and make all the changes  he wants to make the other guy laughter  that make all the changes he wants to  make so by the end of the whole process  we can tell you who wrote the first  draft of each chapter but we can't tell  you who at which sentence what types of  disagreements are there are there  disagreements like I don't believe this  character would do this or I don't  believe this character would maybe say  this in this particular way are there  disagreements about the story arc with  the story arc and what the project is  that's something we talked about a lot  at the beginning of this it's really  important when you're doing this kind of  thing to have  very clear idea of what you both think  the job is because you know if I'm if  I'm writing a comic space opera and he's  writing a recipe book it's not going to  work out and there's a lot of  practically seem to die because people  don't start off on the same place so we  had a lot of conversation at the  beginning about how we wanted this to  look so we have we have a measure which  there's a question we can say okay does  this get us closer to what we were  aiming for and you know part of the  point of a collaboration is that it's  never going to be what I would have done  by myself if I've written some dialogue  for a character and ty thinks it's wrong  then he should change it and fix it and  make it so we think that's right and as  long as it's in the error bars for me to  then were fun there have been a couple  of times we had disagreements about how  to do a particular scene and with that  we've always kind of had the the base  rule of whichever version is the most  awesome we should do that  it just is vague but but surprisingly  useful so as far as the the story arc of  the television program I mean I've read  that you you have a sense of what the  total arc will be for what I believe  will ultimately is it nine nine books in  the expand series its nine novels and  then a collection of the short stories  so I've read and correct me if I'm wrong  that from the beginning you had a sense  for the total arc of that not just of  each individual book what is or I guess  when I watch other TV shows I often  wonder did they know you know at the end  of season two what the ultimate  conclusion to this series would be and  typically this applies to series that  are not based on on books or a series of  books  what are those conversations like I mean  when you start season one of the TV show  of the expanse and it's not clear  whether this is going to be popular or  it isn't what's the thought process that  goes into where do we leave season one  at its end the  this is let's swing for the fences you  know it's I would love for this to to  get to the complete Ark of the books and  that's what we're aiming for that's you  know we've had this conversation with  Naren our 4runner and what we're going  to try to do is actually the whole thing  and I might I can't speak to Naren or ty  or what their touch downs are but mine  is Babylon 5 which is a wreck 15 that  119 parts Babylon 5 is to my mind one of  the touchstones  for long arc television it's under  appreciated that way but the things that  happen in season 5 season 4 and season 5  are set up in the pilot episode it was a  tremendous act of faith in the creative  process and in the the support of  television for this kind of storytelling  and you know there were compromises  along the way because life intrudes but  overall I think it was a success and  that's the that's the model  I would like to embrace let's dig in a  little bit to the science I mean there's  just so much science of different types  I mean we're talking about physics  spacefaring technology very very long  list what's the process for  familiarizing yourself enough with both  the vocabulary and the concepts to then  be able to integrate it into the  storyline well it helps if you've been a  geek for 20 years before you start okay  that was kind of the past at the time I  both took Thai doesn't have a degree in  science but he studied astronomy and all  the kind of the bodies in the solar  system and and cosmology just as a  educated layman for a very long time  which was part of why I got involved in  the project I solved the the three-ring  binder with all of his research and it  figured out he already knew a bunch of  this stuff because interested him my  degree is in biology I have a bachelors  in biology specializing in genetics so I  had that to draw from and then  everything I've read since then which  you know I got the degree a long time  ago so I've read a lot in the meantime  do you know do you do you have people  who contact you to scrutinize the  minutiae of the science absolutely um  that's that's just something that  happens that's the price of paying  things I sometimes we get it wrong we  certainly have done some things that we  knew we were skating fast on fairly thin  ice but at least we you know we  acknowledge that and we try to make one  pass through the book right at the end  where we take out all the numbers so it  gets harder to criticize us hmm what's  the the most difficult thing about the  tv-show process and I don't know you  know now that you're co-executive  producers we talked about maybe this  problem tends to go away but one of the  one of the areas that people whose books  have been turned into TV shows or movies  that I hear over and over again it's  difficult is when when there are  elements changed and it's changed in a  way that the writer doesn't like well at  that point you as soon as you've sold  the rights to your story it's no longer  your project in their project and we tie  are a very rare position of being able  to be in there and be part of the the  team and be part of the conversations so  yeah there are there are choices and  here's the thing a lot of times there  are choices that are made that I don't  understand and I think are wrong and I  think are leading us down the wrong path  until I see see the final cut and then I  think oh that's that's why they did that  generally speaking it's a terrible idea  to have the writers in the room yes the  the the tool set that I and that to a  lesser degree I think I brings to the  the room is so steeped in a certain way  of thinking tight a little more fluid  and is thinking that I am it tells it  lends itself to a certain kind of  storytelling so for the last three years  really what I've been doing is getting a  crash course in other kinds of  storytelling and visual storytelling and  watching the things that I know very  well be changed and shaped and  translated into this new medium with  this new toolbox yeah there are there  are a lot of times when I don't  understand exactly what we're doing  until I can see the end result and the  people who were working with we got  lucky we've got a really great team we  got a bunch of people who are doing this  because they like the base material they  like the subject matter and so they want  the spirit of the original property the  books to show up however however changed  it needs to be they want that spirit to  be on the screen when we get there well  the end result is working we've been  talking about the expanse and with half  of the writing team Daniel Abraham great  to talk to you thanks so much it's been  a pleasure

